WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.371
Model quantization represents weights, and sometimes activations or caches,

00:00:03.371 --> 00:00:04.824
with lower numerical precision.

00:00:04.824 --> 00:00:09.185
A model originally stored with 32-bit or 16-bit floating-point values may be

00:00:09.185 --> 00:00:11.366
converted so many weights use 8,

00:00:11.366 --> 00:00:13.546
6, 5, 4, or fewer bits.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:17.907
The runtime combines those compact values with scales and other metadata to

00:00:17.907 --> 00:00:20.087
approximate the original computations during inference.

00:00:20.087 --> 00:00:25.175
Lower precision reduces the bytes that must be stored, loaded, and moved through memory.

00:00:25.175 --> 00:00:29.536
That can make a model fit in limited VRAM or system RAM

00:00:29.536 --> 00:00:32.080
and reduce memory-bandwidth pressure during token generation.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.441
A model that remains fully on an accelerator may run much faster

00:00:36.441 --> 00:00:39.711
than a higher-precision version split between GPU and CPU.

00:00:39.711 --> 00:00:42.619
Smaller files also download and load more quickly.

00:00:42.619 --> 00:00:44.072
The tradeoff is approximation.

00:00:44.072 --> 00:00:46.616
Quantization can alter probabilities and model behavior,

00:00:46.616 --> 00:00:50.614
with effects that depend on architecture, method, bit level, and task.

00:00:50.614 --> 00:00:54.611
Some models tolerate moderate quantization well; aggressive compression may damage reasoning,

00:00:54.611 --> 00:00:56.792
coding, instruction following, or rare knowledge.

00:00:56.792 --> 00:01:00.789
Hardware and runtimes also differ in which formats they accelerate efficiently.

00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:02.606
Quantization is not magic compression.

00:01:02.606 --> 00:01:06.240
It does not remove context-cache memory, runtime overhead, tokenizer files,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.330
or every activation.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:11.328
It does not increase the model's underlying capability or guarantee speed.

00:01:11.328 --> 00:01:16.052
The useful question is whether a particular quantized artifact fits the target system,

00:01:16.052 --> 00:01:18.233
runs efficiently in the selected runtime,

00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:21.140
and preserves acceptable quality for the real workload.
